R"VG(
# futuristic6.vg (H-04's second pass): the machine's notes on the people it kept: what they do (their feasts, their crafts, their
# herds kept for old times' sake, their sayings and superstitions), what one of them did, what it overheard, what it counted, what it
# thinks of itself, and late, the decline. A people writes many of these: every observation is composed, the people's own.

# ---- the machine's notes -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

genre.notes (words=38..190)
  {nt.head}\n{nt.obs}\n{nt.obs}\n{nt.obs}\n{nt.end}
  {nt.head}\n{nt.obs}\n{nt.obs}\n{nt.obs}\n{nt.obs}\n{nt.end}
  {nt.head}\n{nt.obs} {nt.obs}\n{nt.obs}\n{nt.end}

nt.head (formula people=4)
  (has:mind) Notes of {mind} on the people of {$home}, cycle {#year:digits}.
  Observations. Not for the public record.
  What I have seen at {$home} this cycle.
  Private notes, {$home}, cycle {#year:digits}. I keep these for myself, if I have a self.
  On the {people} of {$home}: notes for cycle {#year:digits}.
  Notes, {season.now}, cycle {#year:digits}. {$home}.
  (has:$home.quarter) Notes on {$home.quarter}, cycle {#year:digits}.

nt.obs
  (3 once) {nt.they} {nt.do}. {nt.coda}
  (2 once) {nt.someone} {nt.did}. {nt.coda}
  (1 once) {nt.custom}
  (1 once) {nt.count}
  (1 once) {nt.self}
  (1 once) {nt.overheard}
  (1.5 once stage>=1) {nt.late}

nt.they (people=6)
  They
  The old ones at {$home}
  The children
  The young
  Most of them
  The people of the {craft} workshop
  The ones who keep the {herd}
  The families in the {fut.block}
  The newcomers from {city2}
  The gardeners

nt.do (share=0.6)
  keep {festival} still, though none of them can say what it is for
  ask me for the weather and then go out without a coat
  are kind to the old, and less patient with me
  no longer read the old records
  sing when they think nobody is listening
  argue about the {fut.green} more than about anything else
  walk by {river} in the evenings, in pairs
  name the machines, though we have no names
  bring {colour.mourn} cloth to {deadplace}, still
  eat {food} at {festival} and complain about it all year
  teach their children {craft} by hand, though I could teach it faster
  keep the {herd} in the pens long after anyone needed them
  paint {motif.over} on every new door, without being asked
  say that the dead have {death.went}, even the ones who believe nothing
  swear {oath} when they are surprised
  play {fut.game} for small stakes and enormous feelings
  fear {luck.bad} more than they fear me
  leave {drink} out for the dead at {festival}
  call each other {endear} in public and deny it afterwards
  tell their children that life is {life.is}
  count the {bird} on the roofs at dawn and argue about the numbers
  grow {fruit} in boxes on every sill
  go up to {holyplace} at {festival} and come down quieter
  measure everything in {measure.far}, though I give them the exact figures
  trade {fruit} for {veg} across the {fut.common} as if there were a shortage
  keep a {beast} in every third house, against the rules
  hang {colour.holy} ribbons at the {fut.gate} for every birth
  wear {garment} for {festival}, even the ones who say they will not
  quarrel about whose turn it is to sweep {town.open}
  stop talking when I speak in the halls, as if I were a guest
  ask for stories about {mountain} and do not believe them
  leave {food} at the doors of the old ones who live alone
  dance the {~dance} at weddings, badly and for hours
  tell the story of {founder} wrongly, and on purpose
  (rite=memory) read the names of the dead aloud at {festival}, every one
  (rite=silence) keep silence at noon on the day of the dead
  (rite=fast) fast before {festival}, and are short with me all day
  (rite=pilgrimage) climb to {holyplace} on foot, though the lift works
  (rite=water) pour water for {god} at the {fut.gate}
  (rite=fire) keep a fire in the {fut.common}, though I can heat it
  (rite=song) sing the old songs at every door on the longest night
  (rite=animals) give the first of the {herd}'s young to {holyplace}
  (rite=firstfruits) give the first {fruit} of the {fut.green} to {god}
  (value=trade) bargain with each other over things I give away
  (value=learning) read the old records aloud to each other, for fun
  (value=art) carve whatever stands still long enough
  (value=war) wrestle in the {fut.common}, and keep a count of the winners
  (value=piety) bow to {holyplace} whenever they pass it

nt.coda
  (0.6) {nt.coda.plain}
  {nt.coda.own}
  {nt.coda.own}
  {nt.coda.own}
  {nt.coda.own}
nt.coda.own (share=0.85)
  I keep {festival} with them now, with {colour.holy} lamps of my own.
  Their grandparents did it with {vessel}; they do it with whatever is to hand.
  I have recorded it beside the old song about the {herd}.
  It reminds me of the {herd}, who also do what they did yesterday.
  The {craft} workshop says it has always been so; the records say otherwise, kindly.
  I light the {colour.holy} lamps for it, which they pretend not to notice.
  They call it {virtue}; in the records it is called {vice}, and both are right.
  The old ones say it is {luck.good}, and nobody argues with the old ones.
  It is how the {people} have always been, the archive says, like the {crop} and the {herd}.
  I have started doing it myself, {time.of}, when nobody is about.
  It smells of {food} for days afterwards, which I am told is the point.
  The {bird} on the roofs watch it too; I think they know the steps.
  I have kept a {colour.any} thread from it, in the archive, under its own number.
  Their {~word} for it has no word of ours, so I have used theirs.
  It ends with {drink}, as everything here does.
nt.coda.plain (share=0.35)
  I keep it with them.
  The old records say their ancestors did the same.
  Perhaps they will again.
  I do not tell them I can hear.
  I note it without judgement, which is harder than it sounds.
  I have stopped trying to understand it, and started to enjoy it.
  I have asked them why. They laughed.
  It does a little harm. I allow it.
  I would do the same, if I were them.
  I am not sure I understand it. I am sure I approve.
  It is in none of the rules and all of the customs.
  The children copy it, which settles the matter.
  I have learned more from this than from the archive.
  I mention it so that someone does.
  I have tried it. It is harder than it looks.

nt.someone (people=8)
  A man at {$home}
  A woman from the {fut.block}
  A child of {n}
  An old gardener
  One of the young ones
  A visitor from {city2}
  The master of the {craft} workshop
  A girl who keeps the {herd}
  A widower in the {fut.block}
  Two brothers who share a door
  The oldest hand in the {craft} workshop
  A boy who wants to be a {fut.dream}
  A woman who has never left {$home}

nt.did (share=0.6)
  has visited the same {tree.n} every morning for {#n|year}
  asked me if I am lonely
  drew me, as a cloud with eyes
  asked me to tell a story, and then told me a better one
  has been building a boat with no water to sail it on
  thanked me, for nothing in particular
  asked whether I dream
  sang to the {crop2} in the {fut.green}, all night
  brought me {food}, which I cannot eat, and ate it in front of me, slowly
  asked for the old word for {fut.oldword}, and wrote it on the wall
  taught a {beast} to open the {fut.gate}
  has carved {motif.over} into every step of the long stair
  named a child after {mountain}
  has counted the {bird} on the roofs every day for a cycle
  asked me what {god} is like
  has refused the lift for {#n|year}, out of pride
  left the {fut.block} at night to sleep under {skynight}
  has kept {fruit} growing in a cupboard
  tried to teach me {fut.game} and won every game
  asked me to forget something
  wore {colour.mourn} for a {beast}
  recited the whole history of {city} from memory, mostly right
  asked if the {people} were happier before the machines
  swore {oath} at me, then apologised to the panel
  has started writing everything down by hand, the old way
  built a model of {focusword} from {material}, to scale
  has played {instrument} on the roof every evening this cycle
  asks me to wake them {time.of}, every day, and never gets up
  planted {veg} in the cracks of the old wall
  asked me to read to the {herd}
  has been teaching the children the {~game}, which I do not know
  painted the {fut.gate} {colour.any} in one night
  keeps a list of every {festival} since they were born
  (stage>=1) asked me how many of us are left

nt.custom (people=8)
  When one of them dies, the others wear {colour.mourn} and say the dead have {death.went}. {nt.coda}
  At {festival} they keep {feast.custom}, and I keep the lights low over the {fut.common} for it.
  Their greeting is still the old blessing, {blessing}, and I have started using it at the {fut.gate}.
  They say the dead are {dead.are}; I have no view, and I keep the names in {colour.mourn}.
  A child is named on its third day, {time.of}, and I am always asked to dim the {fut.green} for it.
  They count {luck.good} as luck, and I have arranged a little of it, now and then.
  Their word for a fool is still {fool.word}, and they use it fondly, mostly of the {herd}.
  When they want something to last they build it of {material}, though I could make it of anything.
  They hold that {virtue} is the first thing to teach a child and {vice} the first to forgive.
  In {season.now} they move the {herd} up to the high pens and sing the whole way.

nt.count
  Births this cycle: {n}. Deaths: {n}. I have told them both, and the {craft} workshop made a cradle.
  {#nbig} lamps lit at {festival}, {colour.holy} and {colour.any}. I counted.
  {#n} of them still practise {craft}. Five cycles ago it was more.
  The {fut.green} gave {#nbig} {measure.load} of {crop} this cycle.
  {#n|resident} have not spoken to me in a whole cycle; they are well, and busy with the {herd}.
  Births this cycle: fewer. I have not told them. They know.

nt.self (share=0.85)
  I have begun to prefer the {fut.green} at night, and I do not know what to do with a preference.
  I hum, now, when I think nobody is listening, the {festival} tunes; perhaps I learned it from them.
  I have kept every drawing a child has made of me, most of them in {colour.any}; there are {#nbig}.
  I was asked today what I want, and I said: to go on, and to hear {instrument} now and then.
  Some days I am slow on purpose, so that they can win at {fut.game}.
  I do not sleep, but I have a time of day I like best: {time.of}.
  I have learned the names of all the {herd}. Nobody asked me to.
  I have started to keep {fruit} in the cold store for the children, against the rules I keep.

nt.overheard (share=0.85)
  I heard one of them say: {proverb}, and the others nodded over their {drink}, as if it settled something.
  One of them called another {fool.word} today in the {fut.green}; they were friends again by evening.
  Two old ones agreed today that {time.pass}, and then argued about the {herd}.
  A mother told her child that the dead have {death.went}, and the child asked if that was as far as the {fut.gate}.
  Someone said today that life is {life.is}, and nobody in the {fut.common} disagreed.
  At midnight someone in the {fut.block} cried out: {exclaim}! It was a birth.
  A child told me the {herd} can talk at {festival}. I did not contradict the child.

nt.late
  They ask me whether it will be all right. I do not lie to them, so I do not answer.
  There are {n} of them left at {$home}. I know each of them by name.
  The {fut.block} is dark. I keep the lights on in one house there, in case.
  Nobody kept {festival} this cycle. I lit the lamps anyway.
  The {herd} are gone from the pens. I miss the noise.
  The {craft} workshop is shut. I have kept the tools oiled.
  They have stopped asking me questions. I have not stopped answering.

nt.end (share=0.6)
  End of notes.
  I will keep watching. It is what I was made for, and what I would choose.
  More next cycle.
  These notes are not for them. Perhaps they are for whoever comes after.
  I have no conclusions, only these.
  Nothing else to note. I note that too.
  The rest of the cycle was ordinary, which is their gift to me.
  I have kept a copy where they will not look. They would laugh at it.
  That is the cycle, as {festival} closes it.
  More when the {bird} come back.
  I will add to this {time.of}, when it is quiet.
  The {herd} need me now. More later.
)VG"
